====================================================================================================
3. THE BUTTONS — shift order, polarity, and the pairs the car is tuned for
====================================================================================================
  [ok ] the shift order is the guide's A, B, Home, Down, Left, Right, Up, Aux1, active-low
        bits: a=7, b=6, home=5, down=4, left=3, right=2, up=1, aux1=0
        a sampled 0 is pressed, which is the active-low comparison in that line
        Start: b.start = digitalRead(PIN_BTN_START) == LOW;

  what the four buttons do (badge_controller.ino padManeuver, first match wins):
      START  -> EmergencyStop
      DOWN   -> Stop
      UP     -> Forward
      LEFT   -> Left
      RIGHT  -> Right
  [ok ] the four drive buttons are forward, left, right and stop, and a stop wins
        precedence: EmergencyStop > Stop > Forward > Left > Right
        start and down both stop; a held direction cannot outvote either
  [ok ] the badge's drive pairs are the phone app's tuned pairs, not new numbers
        DRIVE_FORWARD_LEFT: badge 180 / app 180
        DRIVE_FORWARD_RIGHT: badge 128 / app 128
        DRIVE_LEFT_LEFT: badge -205 / app -205
        DRIVE_LEFT_RIGHT: badge 190 / app 190
        DRIVE_RIGHT_LEFT: badge 205 / app 205
        DRIVE_RIGHT_RIGHT: badge -195 / app -195
  [ok ] the guide's pins are the ones this firmware uses
        badge_pins.h is a transcription of the guide's section 2 table

====================================================================================================
4. PAIRING AND THE DEADMAN — who may talk, and what happens when nobody does
====================================================================================================
  [ok ] an ESP-NOW command lands on the same ctrl_left/ctrl_right and heartbeat_time the phone uses
        ctrl_left = constrain(left, -255, 255);
        ctrl_right = constrain(right, -255, 255);
        heartbeat_time = millis();
  [ok ] a learned MAC is registered as a peer before anything is unicast to it
        badge side, learning the car: addPeer(gCarMac);
        car side, learning the badge: espnow_add_peer(espnow_badge_mac);
        ESP-NOW refuses esp_now_send() to an address that is not in the peer table, so a missing
        registration is a link that dies at the moment it pairs - and it compiles perfectly.
  [ok ] the phone's cutoff is still the only stop rule, and it is untouched
        the 750 ms cutoff zeroes ctrl_left/ctrl_right whichever controller last spoke
        a badge that goes quiet for 750 ms therefore stops the car by itself

====================================================================================================
The badge and the car agree on the wire, and the badge can draw what it says.

