FLASH (transcribed from the upload run; the tool output is not re-capturable now that the board is gone)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  .tools\arduino.cmd upload -p COM8 --fqbn "esp32:esp32:esp32c3:CDCOnBoot=cdc,FlashMode=dio,FlashFreq=80,FlashSize=4M,PartitionScheme=no_ota" remote/badge_controller

  Writing at 0x000c1939... (100 %)
  Wrote 742496 bytes (451632 compressed) at 0x00010000 in 2.5 seconds (effective 2398.1 kbit/s)...
  Hash of data verified.
  Leaving...
  Hard resetting via RTS pin...
  New upload port: COM8 (serial)

What that proves: the ESP32-C3 ROM bootloader accepted the image, wrote it at 0x10000 and verified the
hash, with no manual download-mode hold (the guide's warning covers boards that need it; this one
resets over USB-Serial-JTAG). The app is 742,496 bytes, inside the 2 MB no_ota app partition.

FLASH TIME: 2026-09-20T07:04:16-04:00

AFTER THE FLASH
The board stopped enumerating before a monitor could attach, so nothing was read back:

  $ .tools\arduino15\packages\esp32\tools\esptool_py\4.5.1\esptool.exe --port COM8 chip_id
  A fatal error occurred: Could not open COM8, the port doesn't exist

  $ powershell -NoProfile -Command "Get-PnpDevice -PresentOnly | ? { $_.InstanceId -like '*VID_303A*' }"
  (no Espressif device present; COM8 is a stale registry entry - getportnames() still lists it)

So: flashed and verified, then unplugged (or its USB dropped). The sketch's own output - the SC7A20
probe, the ESP-NOW init line, the [cmd]/[inject] lines - has never been read, and neither the panel
nor the LEDs have been looked at. A serial monitor is left retrying on COM8, so plugging the badge
back in should attach and capture it.

